The commands a release host answers with, standing in for the real ones while
deploy/release.sh runs for real in a test.

These are files in the repository rather than scripts a test writes into a
temporary directory, and that is the whole point of the directory. macOS scans
every newly created executable the first time it is executed: about a quarter
of a second each, once per file, cached afterwards for the life of that file.
A test that wrote five of these per case paid that scan ten times a run — some
1.4 s per case, which under a full `pnpm test` with every core busy grew past
vitest's five-second default and made the suite look flaky. Checked in, the
scan happens once per checkout and never again.

Each of them is parameterised by environment rather than by being generated
with paths baked in; release-retention.test.ts sets them:

  FAKE_DOCKER_LOG        every docker argument list is appended here
  FAKE_ENV_FILE          the server .env path `stat` expects to be asked about
  FAKE_MARKER            the .coinslot-revision file `docker image prune` reads
  FAKE_VERIFIED_MARKER   the exact marker line that must be in it by then
  FAKE_PRUNE_FAILURE     "1" makes `docker image prune` fail
